Map the exit codes of commands run in parallel as for other commands.
With -P, xargs used the raw Windows exit code as the command's status.
A code of 0x80000000 or more, such as 0xC0000005 for an access
violation, was negative and taken for a failure to start the command:
xargs printed a message with a stale errno and exited with 126 or 127.
A command ended by busybox kill, whose code has the signal number in
its top byte, was reported as killed by a huge signal number, and other
codes above 255 were not reduced as they are without -P.

Convert the code with exit_code_to_wait_status(), as waitpid() does for
the commands xargs runs one at a time, and return the status in the
form wait4pid() uses.

---
 findutils/xargs.c |    9 +++++++--
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

--- a/findutils/xargs.c
+++ b/findutils/xargs.c
@@ -226,7 +226,7 @@
 #if ENABLE_FEATURE_XARGS_SUPPORT_PARALLEL && ENABLE_PLATFORM_MINGW32
 static int wait_for_slot(int *idx)
 {
-	int i;
+	int i, wstat;
 
 	/* if less than max_procs running, set status to 0, return next free slot */
 	if (G.running_procs < G.max_procs) {
@@ -246,7 +246,12 @@
 			*idx = G.running_procs - 1;
 			if (!G.max_procs)
 				G.running_procs--;
-			return status;
+			/* Map the exit code as waitpid() does, and return the
+			 * status in the form wait4pid() uses */
+			wstat = exit_code_to_wait_status(status);
+			if (WIFSIGNALED(wstat))
+				return WTERMSIG(wstat) + 0x180;
+			return WEXITSTATUS(wstat);
 		}
 	}
 
